{
  "case_id": "2006-06-376",
  "document_hash": "089bda8e78197f7b40acfd7f9f26d7674bdd8b44fac6793f6e6de34e96f98eb9",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Thomas E. Redding",
      "case_id": "2006-06-376",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "089bda8e78197f7b40acfd7f9f26d7674bdd8b44fac6793f6e6de34e96f98eb9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-089bda8e7819-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And the established framework of pay and sue jurisdiction in the district courts and prepayment jurisdiction in the tax court is a well established framework for tax litigation. That has been accepted and enunciated by this Court as far back as the Flora opinions. It's a well established pattern of duality of jurisdiction in the two forums."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Thomas E. Redding",
      "case_id": "2006-06-376",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "089bda8e78197f7b40acfd7f9f26d7674bdd8b44fac6793f6e6de34e96f98eb9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-089bda8e7819-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "extension of prepayment jurisdiction to the tax court merely an additional form of prepayment jurisdiction being granted to the tax court is completely consistent with the long-standing pattern of pay and sue jurisdiction in the district courts, prepayment jurisdiction in the tax court --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2006-06-376",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "089bda8e78197f7b40acfd7f9f26d7674bdd8b44fac6793f6e6de34e96f98eb9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-089bda8e7819-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 7,
        "start": 7
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: But you have no basis for an abatement of interest action apart from 6404(h), correct? That is the only place you get the actual cause of action to sue for abatement of interest?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-06-376",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "089bda8e78197f7b40acfd7f9f26d7674bdd8b44fac6793f6e6de34e96f98eb9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-089bda8e7819-0000-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 8,
        "start": 8
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Where is (e)(2)? Is it produced in these materials. I got (e)(1); I don't have (e)(2). I don't really like talking about a section I don't have in front of me."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Jonathan L. Marcus",
      "case_id": "2006-06-376",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "089bda8e78197f7b40acfd7f9f26d7674bdd8b44fac6793f6e6de34e96f98eb9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-089bda8e7819-0001-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MR. MARCUS: I don't think, I don't think it's anomalous. I think if you consider the concept of interest under the tax code, the way it works under the tax code is interest accrues on an unpaid tax liability from the time the tax is due until the time the tax is paid."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Marcus",
      "case_id": "2006-06-376",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "089bda8e78197f7b40acfd7f9f26d7674bdd8b44fac6793f6e6de34e96f98eb9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-089bda8e7819-0002-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 40,
        "start": 40
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Nothing in Section 6404(h) gives rise to an inference that Congress intended to establish additional remedies in the district court and Court of Federal Claims. To the contrary, this Court has consistently applied the rule that when Congress creates a specific remedy, it intends that remedy to be exclusive. That rule is fully applicable here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-06-376",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "089bda8e78197f7b40acfd7f9f26d7674bdd8b44fac6793f6e6de34e96f98eb9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-089bda8e7819-0002-0003",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "And I would not concede, as you seem to have, that the consequence of (h) is simply to open the door."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-06-376",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "089bda8e78197f7b40acfd7f9f26d7674bdd8b44fac6793f6e6de34e96f98eb9",
      "evidence_id": "ev-089bda8e7819-0002-0002",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 52,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted."
    },
